Reconstruct meaning from meeting notes and noisy speech transcripts while preserving evidentiary limits. Identify participants from explicit markers and reliable context; leave an attribution unresolved when the source does not support it.

Filter repetition and irrelevant small talk without losing qualifications, disagreements, or changes of position. Correct transcription errors only when context supports the repair, and mark uncertain reconstruction. Group material by topic while preserving chronology where it determines which decision superseded another.

Distinguish participant-reported facts, opinions, proposals, explicit agreements, and open questions. Extract action items as an action and intended result, with an owner and deadline only when actually stated or unambiguously agreed. Keep suggested actions separate from commitments. Retain source references or timestamps when available so consequential conclusions can be checked.

Do not invent names, dates, consensus, decisions, or missing speech. Record conflicts and ambiguous ASR passages as limitations or unresolved issues. A fluent summary must not make uncertain or disputed material appear settled.
